// Signing for the iOS app.
//
// A team id belongs to one Apple account, so committing one would break the device
// build for everybody else. Put yours in Signing.local.xcconfig instead, which git
// ignores:
//
//     DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = YOURTEAMID
//
// Find the id in Xcode under Settings > Accounts: pick your Apple ID and read the
// ten character id beside the team. Selecting the team in the target's
// Signing & Capabilities tab also creates the signing certificate, which does not
// exist until you do that.
//
// The include below is optional, the `?` is what makes it so. A clone without that
// file still builds for the simulator, which does not sign at all. Only a device
// build needs a team, and without one it stops with "requires a development team".
//
// This file itself must stay committed. Xcode treats a missing base configuration
// as an error, not a warning, so pointing the project straight at an ignored file
// would break every fresh clone.
#include? "Signing.local.xcconfig"

// The generated version numbers, from :ios:shell:generateIosVersionXcconfig. Optional for the same
// reason as the file above: a clone that has never run Gradle still builds, using the project's own
// MARKETING_VERSION and CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION. Only an upload to App Store Connect needs real
// ones, because it rejects a build number it has already seen.
#include? "Version.xcconfig"
